1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,859
How Git stores data?

2
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:14,672
Under every command you have used, Git stores only four kinds of objects in .git/objects,

3
00:00:14,672 --> 00:00:17,777
each named by the hash of its content:

4
00:00:17,777 --> 00:00:24,477
blobs (file contents), trees (folders), commits (snapshots with history) and tags.

5
00:00:24,477 --> 00:00:25,784
Plus references:

6
00:00:25,784 --> 00:00:30,114
small files with names like main pointing to commits.

7
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:36,266
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

8
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:40,361
Follow the chain from main down to a file's content:

9
00:00:40,361 --> 00:00:42,279
The commit points to a tree.

10
00:00:42,279 --> 00:00:46,114
The tree lists names and the blobs holding the contents:

11
00:00:46,667 --> 00:00:48,695
The commit points to a tree.

12
00:00:48,695 --> 00:00:52,751
The tree lists names and the blobs holding the contents:

13
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:55,595
The ID is the content's hash:

14
00:00:55,595 --> 00:00:59,869
Git computes the same ID for the same bytes, anywhere.

15
00:01:01,633 --> 00:01:03,370
Let me try something.

16
00:01:03,370 --> 00:01:09,488
Corrupt an object file (simulating a disk error), then use the repository:

17
00:01:10,033 --> 00:01:21,626
The object file no longer decompresses to content whose hash matches its name; Git detects it (every object is verified by its hash) and refuses to use it.

18
00:01:21,626 --> 00:01:24,393
git fsck checks the whole repository:

19
00:01:26,133 --> 00:01:28,323
A blob is defined by its content:

20
00:01:28,323 --> 00:01:33,034
write the same content again and Git recreates exactly the same object.

21
00:01:33,034 --> 00:01:40,333
In real life you get the content back from another clone (git fetch from the remote restores missing objects).

22
00:01:42,067 --> 00:01:42,780
Got it.

23
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:45,531
What do I need to remember?

24
00:01:46,067 --> 00:01:46,968
Objects:

25
00:01:46,968 --> 00:01:55,306
blob (content), tree (folder), commit (snapshot + parents + message), tag.

26
00:01:55,867 --> 00:02:01,881
Every object's name is the hash of its content; same content, same object.

27
00:02:02,433 --> 00:02:08,638
References (branches, tags, HEAD) are names pointing to commits.

28
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,452
I keep hearing about Git objects.

29
00:02:13,452 --> 00:02:15,209
What is it, really?

30
00:02:15,767 --> 00:02:20,310
We build a commit by hand with plumbing commands (hash-object,

31
00:02:20,310 --> 00:02:24,047
update-index, write-tree, commit-tree, update-ref),

32
00:02:24,047 --> 00:02:29,103
to see that git add and git commit are nothing more than these steps.

33
00:02:29,103 --> 00:02:32,181
git cat-file lets us inspect every result.

34
00:02:33,933 --> 00:02:38,333
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

35
00:02:38,867 --> 00:02:42,094
Change a file and store its content as a blob:

36
00:02:42,094 --> 00:02:44,971
Stage it and write a tree from the index:

37
00:02:45,533 --> 00:02:48,295
Stage it and write a tree from the index:

38
00:02:48,295 --> 00:02:52,808
Create the commit object with main as parent, then move main to it:

39
00:02:53,367 --> 00:02:58,429
Create the commit object with main as parent, then move main to it:

40
00:02:58,429 --> 00:03:02,358
A normal commit, made without git add or git commit.

41
00:03:02,358 --> 00:03:03,869
git status is clean:

42
00:03:03,869 --> 00:03:06,891
index, working directory and main agree.

43
00:03:08,633 --> 00:03:10,225
Let me try something.

44
00:03:10,225 --> 00:03:14,243
Create a commit object but forget to move the branch:

45
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:19,732
The commit exists in the object store, but no reference points to it,

46
00:03:19,732 --> 00:03:23,521
so git log (which starts from HEAD) does not show it.

47
00:03:23,521 --> 00:03:29,454
git status shows the staged change as still uncommitted, because HEAD did not move:

48
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:36,249
Move the branch to the commit (what git commit would have done as its last step):

49
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:38,713
Got it.

50
00:03:38,713 --> 00:03:41,465
What do I need to remember?

51
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:50,095
git add = store the blob + update the index; git commit = write a tree + create a commit + move the branch.

52
00:03:50,633 --> 00:03:53,573
git cat-file inspects any object.

53
00:03:54,133 --> 00:03:58,928
An object without a reference is invisible to git log but still in the store.

54
00:04:00,667 --> 00:04:02,586
How does HEAD work?

55
00:04:03,133 --> 00:04:05,457
HEAD is the answer to "where am I?".

56
00:04:05,457 --> 00:04:09,716
Normally it points to a branch, and the branch points to a commit.

57
00:04:09,716 --> 00:04:13,782
That indirection is why committing moves the branch you are on,

58
00:04:13,782 --> 00:04:17,073
and switching branches changes what HEAD points to.

59
00:04:18,833 --> 00:04:23,233
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

60
00:04:23,767 --> 00:04:24,563
Switch:

61
00:04:24,563 --> 00:04:26,611
only HEAD changes.

62
00:04:27,167 --> 00:04:27,829
Switch:

63
00:04:27,829 --> 00:04:29,533
only HEAD changes.

64
00:04:29,533 --> 00:04:30,196
Commit:

65
00:04:30,196 --> 00:04:34,551
the branch moves, HEAD still names the branch.

66
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:35,700
Commit:

67
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:39,639
the branch moves, HEAD still names the branch.

68
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:43,035
Let me try something.

69
00:04:43,035 --> 00:04:50,119
Edit .git/HEAD to point to a branch that does not exist (as a broken script or typo would):

70
00:04:50,667 --> 00:04:53,162
On branch mian … No commits yet:

71
00:04:53,162 --> 00:05:00,805
Git believes you are on a brand-new, empty branch, because HEAD names a branch that has no commit.

72
00:05:00,805 --> 00:05:02,053
Nothing is lost:

73
00:05:02,053 --> 00:05:04,236
your branches are untouched.

74
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:12,369
Point HEAD back to a real branch (the safe way, with the command instead of editing the file):

75
00:05:14,133 --> 00:05:14,847
Got it.

76
00:05:14,847 --> 00:05:17,598
What do I need to remember?

77
00:05:18,133 --> 00:05:23,418
HEAD → branch → commit; committing moves the branch, switching moves HEAD.

78
00:05:23,967 --> 00:05:28,526
HEAD~N, HEAD^2, @ navigate from there.

79
00:05:29,067 --> 00:05:32,421
git symbolic-ref reads and sets HEAD safely.

80
00:05:34,167 --> 00:05:37,051
Why would I need branches are references?

81
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:39,917
A branch is not a copy of anything:

82
00:05:39,917 --> 00:05:44,749
it is a 41-byte file (or a line in packed-refs) containing one commit ID.

83
00:05:44,749 --> 00:05:49,316
Creating a branch is instant, deleting one deletes only that pointer,

84
00:05:49,316 --> 00:05:53,684
and a "branch's commits" are simply the commits reachable from it.

85
00:05:55,433 --> 00:05:59,833
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

86
00:06:00,367 --> 00:06:05,771
Create a branch by writing the reference yourself; Git sees a normal branch:

87
00:06:06,333 --> 00:06:11,738
Create a branch by writing the reference yourself; Git sees a normal branch:

88
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:15,045
Let me try something.

89
00:06:15,045 --> 00:06:18,944
Delete a branch reference and look for "its" commits:

90
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:24,873
The commit "Add green tea to the menu" no longer appears in git log --all:

91
00:06:24,873 --> 00:06:26,470
nothing references it.

92
00:06:26,470 --> 00:06:27,632
It still exists:

93
00:06:27,632 --> 00:06:31,189
deleting a branch deleted a pointer, not commits.

94
00:06:32,933 --> 00:06:39,728
Recreate the reference to the same commit (the ID was in the earlier output, or in git reflog):

95
00:06:41,467 --> 00:06:42,180
Got it.

96
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:44,931
What do I need to remember?

97
00:06:45,467 --> 00:06:47,313
A branch is a reference:

98
00:06:47,313 --> 00:06:49,621
a name containing a commit ID.

99
00:06:50,167 --> 00:06:55,021
Creating, moving and deleting branches only changes these pointers.

100
00:06:55,567 --> 00:07:00,121
Unreferenced commits stay in the object store until garbage collection.

101
00:07:01,867 --> 00:07:04,561
What should I know about detached HEAD?

102
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:10,362
"Detached HEAD" means HEAD points directly to a commit instead of to a branch.

103
00:07:10,362 --> 00:07:14,949
It happens when you check out a tag, a commit ID or a remote branch.

104
00:07:14,949 --> 00:07:21,289
Looking around is perfectly fine; commits made there belong to no branch and are easy to lose.

105
00:07:23,033 --> 00:07:27,433
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

106
00:07:27,967 --> 00:07:29,566
Let's run it.

107
00:07:30,100 --> 00:07:33,921
.git/HEAD contains a commit ID, not ref:

108
00:07:33,921 --> 00:07:35,354
refs/heads/....

109
00:07:35,354 --> 00:07:41,467
Inspecting old code, running old tests, building an old release:

110
00:07:41,467 --> 00:07:42,804
all fine here.

111
00:07:44,567 --> 00:07:46,235
Let me try something.

112
00:07:46,235 --> 00:07:51,161
Make a hotfix commit while detached, then switch back to main:

113
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:52,390
Git warns:

114
00:07:52,390 --> 00:07:55,288
the commit is not connected to any branch,

115
00:07:55,288 --> 00:08:00,463
so no branch log shows it and garbage collection will eventually delete it.

116
00:08:00,463 --> 00:08:03,706
The warning even prints the command to keep it.

117
00:08:03,706 --> 00:08:07,569
If the terminal output is gone, the reflog still has it:

118
00:08:09,333 --> 00:08:11,133
Here is the fix.

119
00:08:12,867 --> 00:08:13,580
Got it.

120
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:16,331
What do I need to remember?

121
00:08:16,867 --> 00:08:24,556
Detached HEAD = HEAD points at a commit, not a branch; normal when inspecting tags or CI checkouts.

122
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:28,404
Commits made while detached belong to no branch:

123
00:08:28,404 --> 00:08:30,744
git switch -c NAME before leaving.

124
00:08:31,300 --> 00:08:32,939
Lost one anyway?

125
00:08:32,939 --> 00:08:36,319
git reflog + git branch NAME SHA.
